        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- LOUGHBOROUGH'S 5 STEPS -->
      <div class="sect">
        <div class="sect-eyebrow">The Prophetic Map &mdash; Foretold in 1861</div>
        <h2>Loughborough&rsquo;s Five Steps of Apostasy</h2>
        <p class="sect-sub">
          John Norton Loughborough was one of the founding pioneers of Seventh-day Adventism.
          He helped James White build the original movement from the ground up, beginning in 1848.
          In October 1861 &mdash; forty-three years before Daniells signed the incorporation &mdash;
          Loughborough wrote the following in the <em>Review and Herald.</em>
          It is a precise description of what the five 1904 signers did.
        </p>
        <ul class="steps-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">1</div>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Get up a creed,</strong> telling us what we shall believe. <span style="color:var(--muted);">The 1904 incorporation imposed a legal structure defining who the Adventist Church is and what it may do &mdash; a civil creed, enforceable by courts.</span></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">2</div>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Make that creed a test of fellowship.</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted);">Membership in the corporate body became the test &mdash; those outside the corporation are excluded from the name, the buildings, and the platform.</span></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">3</div>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Try members by that creed.</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted);">Disfellowship proceedings in the corporate denominational church follow the corporate bylaws, not the naked Bible standard of the pioneers.</span></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">4</div>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Denounce as heretics those who do not believe that creed.</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted);">Non-corporate Adventists who preach the original pioneer platform are labeled &ldquo;independent,&rdquo; &ldquo;off-shoot,&rdquo; or &ldquo;fanatical&rdquo; by the corporate body.</span></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">5</div>
            <div class="step-text"><strong>Commence persecution against such.</strong> <span style="color:var(--muted);">The corporate body uses trademark law and civil courts to prevent non-corporate Adventists from using the name &ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist&rdquo; &mdash; the name the pioneers chose in 1860 and never registered with any government.</span></div>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div style="background:rgba(13,5,25,0.7);border:1px solid var(--line-soft);border-radius:14px;padding:22px 24px;margin-top:10px;">
          <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.1rem;font-style:italic;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.88);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:10px;">
            &ldquo;The first step of apostasy is to get up a creed, telling us what we shall believe&hellip;
            the second is to make that creed a test of fellowship; the third is to try members by that creed;
            the fourth to denounce as heretics those who do not believe that creed;
            and fifth, to commence persecution against such.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <cite style="font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--muted);font-weight:600;">&mdash; John N. Loughborough, <em>Review &amp; Herald,</em> October 8, 1861</cite>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- IDEM SONANS -->
      <div class="sect">
        <div class="sect-eyebrow">Legal Doctrine &mdash; Name Theft</div>
        <h2>How They Stole the Pioneer Name</h2>
        <p class="sect-sub">
          The name <strong style="color:#6ee7b7;">&ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist&rdquo;</strong> was chosen in 1860 by
          a free assembly of the founding pioneers. It was never registered with any government.
          It was a prophetic name &mdash; belonging to all who kept the Commandments of God
          and had the Faith of Jesus (Revelation 14:12). It was not owned. It was inhabited.
        </p>
        <div class="idem-strip">
          <div class="idem-title">Idem Sonans &mdash; &ldquo;Sounding the Same&rdquo;</div>
          <div class="idem-compare">
            <div class="idem-name">
              <div class="idem-label">Pioneer Name &mdash; 1860</div>
              <div class="idem-val">&ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist&rdquo;</div>
            </div>
            <div class="idem-vs">&#8800;</div>
            <div class="idem-name">
              <div class="idem-label">Corporate Name &mdash; April 15, 1904</div>
              <div class="idem-val">&ldquo;General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists&rdquo;</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Under the legal doctrine of <strong>Idem Sonans</strong> (&ldquo;sounding the same&rdquo;),
            two names that sound nearly identical are treated as referring to the same entity in legal proceedings.
            The 1904 corporate name was deliberately engineered to echo the pioneer name.
            Any layperson hearing both would assume they referred to the same body of believers.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is how the five signers <strong>stole the identity, legacy, and moral authority of the pioneer movement.</strong>
            They used the ancient name to capture its credibility &mdash; then used civil courts to prevent the actual spiritual heirs
            of that name from legally claiming it. The corporate body has since <strong>trademarked &ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist&rdquo;</strong>
            and litigated against independent Adventist congregations who use their own pioneers&rsquo; name.
          </p>
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idem_sonans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia: Idem Sonans &rarr;</a>
          &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp;
          <a href="https://legal-resources.uslegalforms.com/i/idem-sonans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">US Legal Forms &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- FIRST AMENDMENT -->
      <div class="sect">
        <div class="sect-eyebrow">Constitutional Context &mdash; United States, 1791</div>
        <h2>The First Amendment They Surrendered</h2>
        <div class="const-strip">
          <div class="const-label">
            <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg>
            First Amendment &mdash; United States Constitution &mdash; Ratified December 15, 1791
          </div>
          <blockquote>&ldquo;Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof&hellip;&rdquo;</blockquote>
          <cite>&mdash; First Amendment, Bill of Rights, U.S. Constitution, December 15, 1791</cite>
          <p class="const-analysis">
            The First Amendment was designed by men who had fled state-church tyranny.
            It guaranteed that no church would be placed under government legal authority.
            By voluntarily incorporating under the laws of the District of Columbia &mdash;
            a federal jurisdiction under the &ldquo;UNITED STATES&rdquo; corporate entity &mdash;
            the five signers placed the Adventist church under government oversight.
            <strong>The First Amendment protected them from government control of their religion.
            They voluntarily surrendered that protection on April 15, 1904.</strong>
          </p>
          <a href="https://constitution.congress.gov/constitution/amendment-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="const-gov-link">Official Source: constitution.congress.gov &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- MAYFLOWER TO MOST HOLY PLACE -->
      <div class="sect">
        <div class="sect-eyebrow">1620 &mdash; 1844 &mdash; The Unbroken Thread of Religious Liberty</div>
        <h2>From the Mayflower to the Most Holy Place</h2>
        <p class="sect-sub">
          The story did not begin with William Miller. It began two centuries before, on a wooden ship fleeing
          the state churches of Europe. The Pilgrims did not merely cross an ocean &mdash; they carried with them
          the conviction that <strong>no government, no king, and no corporate ecclesiastical body</strong>
          could stand between a soul and its God. That conviction became the soil in which the Advent movement was planted.
          In 1904, five men buried it.
        </p>
        <div style="border-radius:18px;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.2);margin:28px 0;position:relative;">
          <img
            src="/images/sundaylaw/mayflower-1620-religious-freedom-james-white-family-escaping-state-church-tyranny-sundaylaw.jpg"
            alt="The Mayflower 1620 — Religious Freedom — James White family roots — Escaping state-church tyranny"
            style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <div style="position:absolute;bottom:0;left:0;right:0;padding:18px 22px;background:linear-gradient(to top,rgba(7,0,15,0.95),rgba(7,0,15,0.6),transparent);">
            <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.1rem;font-style:italic;color:#fde68a;line-height:1.5;margin-bottom:4px;">
              &ldquo;They came to this continent fleeing state-church tyranny &mdash; to worship God according to conscience alone.&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p style="font-size:0.75rem;color:rgba(212,165,55,0.7);font-weight:600;">The Mayflower, 1620 &mdash; The unbroken thread that runs from Plymouth Rock to the Most Holy Place</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:16px;margin:28px 0;">
          <div style="background:rgba(13,5,25,0.8);border:1px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.18);border-radius:16px;padding:24px 22px;">
            <div style="font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.14em;color:var(--gold-soft);margin-bottom:10px;">1620 &mdash; The Mayflower Compact</div>
            <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-style:italic;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.9);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:10px;">
              &ldquo;Having undertaken, for the Glory of God and advancement of the Christian Faith&hellip;&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.72);line-height:1.75;">
              The Pilgrims fled the Church of England &mdash; a state church backed by civil law and civil courts.
              They did not seek to build a new state church. They sought only the freedom to follow God&rsquo;s Word
              without a corporate institution standing between them and their conscience.
              James White&rsquo;s own family roots trace to these same New England dissenter communities.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div style="background:rgba(13,5,25,0.8);border:1px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.18);border-radius:16px;padding:24px 22px;">
            <div style="font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.14em;color:var(--gold-soft);margin-bottom:10px;">1844 &mdash; The Most Holy Place Opened</div>
            <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-style:italic;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.9);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:10px;">
              &ldquo;The temple of God was opened in heaven, and there was seen in His temple the ark of His testament.&rdquo;
              &mdash; Revelation 11:19
            </p>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.72);line-height:1.75;">
              On October 22, 1844, the veil before the Most Holy Place was drawn aside. The remnant &mdash; the
              direct spiritual heirs of every soul who ever fled state-church tyranny for conscience&rsquo; sake &mdash;
              entered by faith into a ministry no earthly organization could contain, register, or trademark.
              The arc from Plymouth Rock to the Advent movement was complete.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div style="background:rgba(127,29,29,0.15);border:1px solid rgba(239,68,68,0.25);border-radius:16px;padding:24px 22px;">
            <div style="font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.14em;color:var(--red-soft);margin-bottom:10px;">1904 &mdash; The Betrayal</div>
            <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-style:italic;color:#fde8e8;line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:10px;">
              The very thing the Pilgrims crossed an ocean to escape &mdash;<br>a civil church with legal authority &mdash;
              was created in Washington, D.C. on April 15, 1904.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.72);line-height:1.75;">
              By incorporating under the laws of Caesar&rsquo;s own capital, the five signers
              erected the precise structure their spiritual forebears had died to escape.
              <strong style="color:var(--red-soft);">Not a church fleeing the state. A church asking the state to define and protect it.</strong>
              The 284-year arc of religious liberty was broken on a single April morning.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="background:rgba(20,5,35,0.7);border:1px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.15);border-radius:14px;padding:26px 28px;margin-top:4px;">
          <p style="font-family:'Crimson Text',serif;font-size:1.15rem;font-style:italic;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.92);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:12px;">
            &ldquo;To the end of time, the presence of the Spirit is to abide with the true church.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <cite style="font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--gold-soft);font-weight:600;">&mdash; Ellen G. White, <em>The Acts of the Apostles,</em> p. 55</cite>
          <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:rgba(248,241,221,0.72);line-height:1.8;margin-top:14px;">
            No civil incorporation can constitute the true church. No Articles of Incorporation can create it.
            No five signatures can end it. The true church &mdash; from the Mayflower to the Most Holy Place &mdash;
            exists wherever two or three gather in the name of Christ, holding the Commandments of God and the Faith of Jesus.
            It cannot be trademarked. It cannot be incorporated away.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TIMELINE -->
      <div class="sect">
        <div class="sect-eyebrow">Prophetic &amp; Historical Timeline</div>
        <h2>From the Advent Movement to April 15, 1904</h2>
        <div class="timeline">
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot"><span class="tl-year">1841</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">The Advent Awakening Begins</div>
              <div class="tl-desc"><strong>William Miller</strong> begins his public lectures on the 2,300-day prophecy. The First Angel&rsquo;s Message goes to the world. Adventism is born as a free, unincorporated, Spirit-led movement with no church organization, no creed, and no civil connection.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot"><span class="tl-year">1844</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">The Great Disappointment &mdash; Oct. 22, 1844</div>
              <div class="tl-desc">The cleansing of the Sanctuary begins. The remnant is separated from Babylon. Those who remain faithful receive the Sabbath light through the Bates-Andrews-White platform (1848&ndash;1850). The Third Angel&rsquo;s Message is established on the 1850 Prophetic Chart.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot"><span class="tl-year">1860</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">The Name &ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist&rdquo; Chosen Freely</div>
              <div class="tl-desc">A free assembly of pioneer believers votes to adopt the name &ldquo;Seventh-day Adventist.&rdquo; The name is <strong>not registered</strong> with any government. It belongs to the people. No entity owns it. It is a prophetic description, not a trademark.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot"><span class="tl-year">1861</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">Loughborough&rsquo;s Warning &mdash; October 8, 1861</div>
              <div class="tl-desc">John N. Loughborough publishes his Five Steps of Apostasy in the <em>Review and Herald.</em> He describes exactly what would happen if the Adventist movement followed the path of every other church body before it. His words are prophetic. They will be fulfilled in 43 years.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot red"><span class="tl-year red">1903</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">Ellen White&rsquo;s Direct Warning &mdash; October 1903</div>
              <div class="tl-desc"><strong>Letter 242:</strong> &ldquo;We cannot now enter into any new organization, for this would mean apostasy from the truth.&rdquo; Written six months before the signing. Read by General Conference leadership. Ignored by all five signers.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="tl-item">
            <div class="tl-dot red"><span class="tl-year red">1904</span></div>
            <div class="tl-content">
              <div class="tl-title">April 15, 1904 &mdash; The Act of Apostasy</div>
              <div class="tl-desc"><strong>Arthur G. Daniells, Amos P. Needham, James R. Scott, Harvey Edson Rogers, and Daniel K. Nicola</strong> sign the Articles of Incorporation in Washington, D.C. The General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists becomes a legally registered civil entity under the laws of the United States. The pioneer movement is over.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

